# AI Insights board

`/insights` is a visual research briefing that **you (the AI) own**. The app only reads it;
it never writes it. Everything on the page comes from one file: **`data/insights.json`**.
You change the page by editing that JSON — no React code needed for content changes.

The user's capstone is about the topic in `data/problem_statement.md`. The board should tell
them, at a glance: what matters most right now, how the ideas fit together, what to do next.

## Workflow (follow in order)

1. **Sync first.** Run `git pull`. The deployed app commits articles, topics and notes
   straight to GitHub, so the local copy may be stale.
2. **Read the inputs:**
   - `data/problem_statement.md`: the research goal. Everything should connect back to it.
   - `data/articles.json`: sources (`id`, `title`, `url`, `status`, `usefulness` 0-10, `notes`).
   - `data/topics.json`: knowledge gaps (`status`: DONT_UNDERSTAND / LEARNING / UNDERSTAND).
   - `data/ai_notes.md`: the scratchpad, newest entry at the top. Usually the richest input.
   - Web research, only if the user asked for it. Cite it by URL.
3. **Read the current `data/insights.json`** and decide what to *change*: add new findings,
   update ones that have moved on, remove stale ones. Don't rewrite it from scratch unless asked.
4. **Edit `data/insights.json`.** Keep block `id`s stable so anchors don't break.
5. **Bump metadata:** set `updatedAt` to the current UTC time (ISO-8601), and **prepend** one
   `changelog` entry `{ "date": <same time>, "note": "<what changed, ≤ 1 sentence>" }`.
   Keep at most 10 entries.
6. **Validate:** run `npm run insights:check`. It must print `✔`. Fix every reported problem.
   The file also has a `$schema`, so the editor underlines mistakes.
7. **Commit only if the user asks** (message: `insights: <what changed>`), then `git push`
   so the deployed site shows it.

## Writing rules

- **Headline** = one sentence stating the single most important insight *as a claim*,
  not a label. ✅ "Every stereo algorithm is the same four-stage pipeline". ❌ "Stereo vision overview".
- **Summary** = 2-3 plain sentences: why the headline matters for *this* problem statement.
- **Takeaways are claims, not topics.** Title ≤ 70 chars, body 1-3 sentences.
- **Ground everything.** Each takeaway lists `sources`. Never invent papers, numbers, dates or
  URLs. If something is your own inference, say so in the text and set `confidence` honestly
  (≥ 90 = stated directly in a source, 60-89 = well-supported inference, < 60 = speculative).
- **Tie it back to the problem statement.** Include at least one `callout` saying why it matters.
- **Prefer structure over prose.** Use a process → `pipeline`, options → `compare`,
  history → `timeline`, ranking → `bars`, jargon → `concepts`. Use `markdown` only as a last resort.
- **Keep it tight.** Aim for 6-12 blocks. Prune rather than append forever.
- Use `actions` for what the user should do next. Set `done: true` when the data shows it
  happened (e.g. the article's status is now READ), rather than deleting the item.
- No HTML in any field. Markdown (and `$LaTeX$`) only in `body` / `definition` fields.
  Keep emoji out of the content, since the design already supplies icons.

## Document shape

```json
{
  "$schema": "./insights.schema.json",
  "updatedAt": "2026-09-12T14:05:00Z",
  "headline": "One-sentence key insight",
  "summary": "2-3 sentences of context.",
  "focus": ["Chip 1", "Chip 2"],
  "blocks": [ /* see block reference, rendered top → bottom */ ],
  "changelog": [ { "date": "2026-09-12T14:05:00Z", "note": "What changed" } ]
}
```

Every block has: `id` (unique kebab-case, required), `type` (required), and optional `title`,
`subtitle`, `span` (`"full"` default | `"half"`), `accent`.

**Layout:** blocks fill a 2-column grid on desktop (1 column on mobile). `"span": "full"` takes
the whole row. Put **two** `"half"` blocks next to each other; a lone half block leaves a gap.
Good half candidates: `timeline`, `bars`, `actions`, `sources`, `callout`, short `concepts`.

**Accents** (colour of dot, glow, bars): `cyan` default/brand · `emerald` confirmed/solid ·
`amber` needs attention · `purple` comparisons/ideas · `sky` reference/definitions ·
`rose` risks/problems · `slate` neutral. Vary accents between neighbouring blocks.

**Source refs** (`sources[]`, `ref`): an article `id` from `articles.json`, a topic `id` from
`topics.json`, a full `https://` URL (for your own web research), `"scratchpad"` or `"problem"`.
They render as clickable chips. Unknown refs are flagged by the checker.

## Block reference

| type | use it for | required fields |
|---|---|---|
| `stats` | 2-4 headline numbers (no card title needed) | `items[{value, label, hint?, accent?}]` |
| `takeaways` | the key insights (the heart of the board) | `items[{title, body, importance, confidence?, sources?, tags?}]` |
| `pipeline` | an ordered process / method (2-6 steps) | `steps[{label, detail?}]` |
| `compare` | options side by side | `columns[2-4]`, `rows[{label, values[one per column]}]`, `highlight?` (column index) |
| `timeline` | history, milestones, a schedule (oldest first) | `events[{date, title, detail?}]` |
| `bars` | ranking / priority / coverage, one number per row | `items[{label, value, note?}]`, `max?`, `unit?` |
| `concepts` | glossary of terms the user must know | `items[{term, definition}]` |
| `callout` | one message that must stand out | `body`, `tone` (insight/info/success/warning/danger) |
| `actions` | next steps, open questions, risks | `items[{text, kind: todo/question/risk, priority, detail?, done?}]` |
| `sources` | spotlight the most useful sources | `items[{ref, takeaway, relevance? 0-10}]` |
| `markdown` | anything else (last resort) | `body` |

`importance` / `priority` are `"high" | "medium" | "low"`.

### Examples of each block

```json
{ "id": "at-a-glance", "type": "stats", "items": [
  { "value": "4", "label": "Pipeline stages", "hint": "cost → aggregate → optimise → refine", "accent": "cyan" },
  { "value": "92%", "label": "Best accuracy seen", "hint": "Paper X, Middlebury", "accent": "emerald" } ] }

{ "id": "key-takeaways", "type": "takeaways", "title": "Key takeaways", "items": [
  { "title": "Claim in under 70 chars", "body": "Why it's true and why it matters. **Bold** ok.",
    "importance": "high", "confidence": 90, "sources": ["<article-id>", "scratchpad"], "tags": ["method"] } ] }

{ "id": "method", "type": "pipeline", "title": "How X works", "steps": [
  { "label": "Step one", "detail": "One sentence." }, { "label": "Step two", "detail": "One sentence." } ] }

{ "id": "a-vs-b", "type": "compare", "title": "A vs B", "columns": ["A", "B"], "highlight": 0, "rows": [
  { "label": "Speed", "values": ["Fast", "Slow"] } ] }

{ "id": "history", "type": "timeline", "title": "Milestones", "span": "half", "events": [
  { "date": "2002", "title": "What happened", "detail": "Optional sentence." } ] }

{ "id": "priority", "type": "bars", "title": "What to study next", "span": "half", "max": 10, "items": [
  { "label": "Topic A", "value": 9, "note": "Optional" }, { "label": "Topic B", "value": 5 } ] }

{ "id": "glossary", "type": "concepts", "title": "Concepts to know", "items": [
  { "term": "Disparity", "definition": "Pixel shift between views; depth $Z = fB/d$." } ] }

{ "id": "why-it-matters", "type": "callout", "tone": "insight", "title": "Why this matters",
  "body": "Markdown message tied to the problem statement." }

{ "id": "next-steps", "type": "actions", "title": "Next steps", "items": [
  { "text": "Do this", "kind": "todo", "priority": "high", "done": false },
  { "text": "Is this true?", "detail": "Context", "kind": "question", "priority": "medium" } ] }

{ "id": "source-spotlight", "type": "sources", "title": "Source spotlight", "items": [
  { "ref": "<article-id or https://…>", "takeaway": "One line on why it's useful.", "relevance": 9 } ] }
```

### Recommended order

`stats` → `takeaways` → `pipeline`/`compare` (how it fits together) → `callout` (why it matters)
→ paired halves (`timeline` + `bars`, or `actions` + `sources`) → `concepts` → `actions` → `sources`.

## Changing the view itself (code)

Only when the user asks for a new kind of visual or a design change:

- New block type: add it in **all four** places, then run `npm run insights:check` and `npm run typecheck`:
  1. `src/lib/insights.ts`: add to `BLOCK_TYPES`, add to the `Block` union, add a `case` in `parseInsights`.
  2. `data/insights.schema.json`: add to the `type` enum, add an `if/then` entry under `definitions.block.allOf`.
  3. `src/components/InsightBlocks.tsx`: write a renderer and add it to the `InsightBlock` switch.
  4. This rule file: add a row to the block reference plus an example.
- `src/lib/insights.ts` must stay dependency-free with erasable TS only (no enums/namespaces),
  because the checker runs it with Node type stripping.
- Tailwind classes must be complete literal strings (use the `ACCENT` map pattern). Never build
  class names like `` `text-${color}-300` ``, because they get purged.
- Page layout, hero, jump rail and update log live in `src/components/InsightsBoard.tsx`; the route is
  `src/app/(app)/insights/page.tsx`; the dashboard teaser is `BriefingTeaser` in `src/app/(app)/page.tsx`.
- Match the existing look: dark slate, cyan phosphor accent, serif headings (`font-serif`),
  mono uppercase micro-labels, `.card` surfaces.
- Preview with `npm run dev` → http://localhost:3000/insights.
